diff --git a/docs/rst/daemon/configuration/floss.rst b/docs/rst/daemon/configuration/floss.rst
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..40e1415
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/rst/daemon/configuration/floss.rst
@@ -0,0 +1,99 @@
+.. _config_floss:
+
+Floss desktop audio
+===================
+
+This integration uses Floss for Bluetooth. The normal ``main`` profile manages the
+``Audio/Sink`` and ``Audio/Source`` nodes published by ``pw-floss --auto``.
+Floss owns Bluetooth codec encoding and radio transport. PipeWire mixes and
+converts application audio to the negotiated PCM format. WirePlumber applies
+ordinary audio linking, default-node selection and stream-volume policy.
+
+BlueZ monitors and their codec/profile switching code have been removed.
+There is no backend selector or BlueZ fallback. ALSA, video capture and generic
+audio policy remain available.
+
+Automatic profile policy
+------------------------
+
+The deployment starts one automatic bridge in the intended user audio session.
+It discovers connected audio devices through Floss, obtains the actual A2DP
+PCM format from the daemon, and exposes a playback node. A headset advertising
+HFP support also gets a microphone node while A2DP is active; a playback-only
+speaker does not get a microphone.
+
+Selecting the headset microphone for an active recording stream requests HFP.
+When the last active recorder leaves that source, the bridge debounces the idle
+transition before returning to A2DP. Playback and microphone node identities
+remain stable across a profile change. Recording from another source, such as
+a laptop microphone, does not request HFP. A passive level meter does not create
+capture demand. Until the voice transport is ready, the source supplies silence.
+
+The bridge owns this profile decision. WirePlumber does not issue competing
+Floss profile commands: its ordinary links determine whether the headset source
+is active. The bridge supplies session priorities so a newly connected headset
+can become the default when there is no explicit user choice. WirePlumber
+retains an explicitly selected built-in microphone or other device across
+headset reconnection. A voice-only device remains in HFP because it has no
+A2DP capability. Device reconnection, capability changes and Floss daemon replacement
+are handled by the automatic bridge. The bridge currently owns one audio device
+at a time; this is not a multi-headset audio mixer or active-seat arbiter.
+
+Configuration
+-------------
+
+Use the normal ``main`` profile; no Floss-specific WirePlumber profile is needed.
+For a multi-instance setup, the ``policy`` instance manages bridge nodes.
+Remove old ``main-floss`` profile selections and configuration referring to
+``monitor.bluez*`` or ``bluez5.*`` properties, which have no implementation in
+this integration. The system deployment must start Floss and the automatic bridge;
+selecting a WirePlumber profile alone does not start either process.
+
+The existing ``node.features.audio.mono`` setting applies to Floss sinks.
+The optional ``device.routes.mute-on-bluetooth-playback-removed`` setting mutes
+ALSA outputs when a running Floss sink disappears, avoiding unexpected speaker
+playback after disconnection. Floss sinks need no PipeWire Device or card-profile
+ID for this policy. The installed ``floss.conf`` example shows the setting.
+
+Bridge contract
+---------------
+
+The bridge publishes these ordinary device nodes:
+
+======================== ==============================================
+Property                 Value
+======================== ==============================================
+``media.class``          ``Audio/Sink`` or ``Audio/Source``
+``device.api``           ``floss``
+``node.name``            ``floss_output.<address>`` / ``floss_input.<address>``
+``api.floss.address``    Bluetooth device address
+``api.floss.adapter``    HCI adapter index
+``api.floss.profile``    ``auto`` for automatic management
+======================== ==============================================
+
+Colons in the node-name address are replaced by underscores. Per-application
+volume remains software controlled. The source and sink PCM formats may be
+renegotiated during profile changes without replacing the nodes. Floss's
+negotiated format query is authoritative; codec capability flags alone do not
+determine the actual feeder rate or prove that a particular codec is active.
+
+A2DP playback can use Floss's enabled codecs, including AAC when both endpoints
+support it and Floss negotiates it. HFP uses Floss's CVSD/mSBC voice path.
+The bridge does not implement Bluetooth codecs. Real controller/kernel SCO
+transport support is still required for headset microphone audio.
+
+Permissions and lifecycle
+-------------------------
+
+The deployment permits the bridge to call Floss D-Bus methods, receive callbacks,
+access PCM sockets through the ``bluetooth-audio`` group, and own the cooperative
+``org.pipewire.FlossAudio`` bus name. Floss reserves audio using a token bound to
+the actual D-Bus caller and excludes competing operations until native teardown
+completes. WirePlumber configuration grants none of these permissions.
+
+An automatic bridge does not itself prove active-seat ownership. The user-service
+configuration must start it only in the intended audio session; shared or
+multi-seat systems need separate ownership policy. No desktop-side software
+fixture can establish real headset interoperability, RF reliability or controller
+SCO support. The integration's isolated test reports distinguish scripted PCM
+and device capabilities from actual Floss codec and controller tests.
